In this paper, we develop new estimators for FID estimation, alongside a complementary non-asymptotic sample complexity theory for the problem. We focus on the setting where image embedding distributions P P and Q Q are exactly Gaussian, a canonical setting for high-dimensional statistical inference. This setting is particularly natural for FID, which by its very definition as a squared Wasserstein distance between moment-matched Gaussians, assumes the image embeddings are close to a Gaussian (else measuring this distance is ill-posed). Under this assumption, we abstract FID estimation into the following natural parametric statistical estimation problem. Problem 1 . Let 𝛍 , 𝛎 ∈ ℝ d  oldsymbol{\mu}, oldsymbol{
