Sample-Optimal Estimation of the Fréchet Inception Distance Skip to main content Report GitHub Issue × Title: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below: Description: Why HTML? Report Issue Back to Abstract Download PDF Contents Reading mode Colour theme License: CC BY 4.0 arXiv:2610.07114v1 [cs.LG] 05 Oct 2026 Sample-Optimal Estimation of the Fréchet Inception Distance Ziyun Chen † † thanks: University of Washington, ziyuncc@cs.washington.edu Jerry Li † † thanks: University of Washington, jerryzli@cs.washington.edu Kevin Tian † † thanks: University of Texas at Austin, kjtian@cs.utexas.edu Yusong Zhu † † thanks: University of Texas at Austin, zhuys@utexas.edu Abstract The Fréchet Inception Distance (FID) is widely used to evaluate generative models, but its empirical plug-in estimator suffers from finite-sample bias [ 4 , 7 ] . We study the sample complexity n n of estimating FID to error ϵ \epsilon between d d -dimensional Gaussians with bounded mean distance and covariances, when one distribution is known. Our contributions are threefold. 1. We establish tight finite-sample Θ ⁡ ( d 2 n ) \Theta( rac{d^{2}}{n}) bias and Θ ⁡ ( d n + d 2 n 2 ) \Theta( rac{d}{n}+ rac{d^{2}}{n^{2}}) variance bounds for the empirical plug-in estimator, establishing a ≳ d 2 \gtrsim d^{2} sample complexity. 2. To debias the empirical plug-in estimator, we generalize the FID ∞ \mathrm{FID}_{\infty} estimator of [ 7 ] to extrapolation methods of arbitrary order k k . We further prove tight bias and variance bounds of Θ ⁡ ( d k + 2 n k + 1 ) \Theta( rac{d^{k+2}}{n^{k+1}}) and Θ ⁡ ( d n + d 2 n 2 ) \Theta( rac{d}{n}+ rac{d^{2}}{n^{2}}) for any order- k k extrapolation under our framework. 3. We introduce Relative Taylor Debiasing (RTD), a new, computationally efficient FID estimation algorithm using debiasing techniques inspired by U-statistics. We show that RTD achieves an O ⁡ ( d ϵ 2 ) O( rac{d}{\epsilon^{2}}) sample complexity, and prove that this is optimal. We provide a complementary empirical evaluation of our new estimators. Our experiments on synthetic Gaussians validate the predicted residual bias and support the tightness of our bounds. On ImageNet with Inception embeddings, RTD achieves the lowest mean estimation error at the standard 50K sample budget, while our second-order variance-aware extrapolation estimator (VALE 2 ) uses only 10K samples to achieve accuracy comparable to FID ∞ at 50K samples. 1 1 1 Authors are ordered alphabetically. 2 2 2 Our code is available at https://github.com/zys996/sample-optimal-fid-estimation . 1 Introduction A central challenge in generative modeling scholarship is evaluation : that is, how can we quantitatively judge the quality of a model’s generations? Without evaluation, it is difficult to make concrete comparisons between different methods, algorithms, and architectures. Downstream performance can sometimes be used as a proxy for quality, but many settings, notably image generation, where the ultimate goal is to produce high-quality natural images, require metrics that serve as direct proxies for generative quality. While there is no perfect metric for this task, the Fréchet inception distance (FID), introduced in [ 10 ] , has become a standard tool for this purpose. The FID is a measure of distance between distributions P , Q P,Q over image embeddings. Formally, let P P , Q Q be distributions over images passed through a fixed embedding (often the Inception network [ 28 ] ). When P = 𝒩 ⁡ ( 𝝁 , 𝐀 ) P={\mathcal{N}}( oldsymbol{\mu},{\mathbf{A}}) and Q = 𝒩 ⁡ ( 𝝂 , 𝐁 ) Q={\mathcal{N}}( oldsymbol{
