^{2}}{\epsilon^{2}}) sample complexity for W 2 W_{2} estimation, assuming that the covariance spectra are bounded away from zero. This requirement is avoided by [ 11 ] , who give a sample complexity of O ⁡ ( d 5 ϵ 4 ) O( rac{d^{5}}{\epsilon^{4}}) for W 2 2 W_{2}^{2} estimation. Beyond the empirical plug-in estimator, alternative estimators aim to improve sample efficiency: [ 31 , 32 ] develop random matrix corrections for W 2 2 W_{2}^{2} estimation, but establish only asymptotic guarantees. For general smooth nonlinear functionals, extrapolation and Taylor debiasing methods have been analyzed by [ 15 , 14 ] . However, smoothness assumptions do not hold uniformly for W 2 2 W_{2}^{2} estimation when singular covariances are allowed, and the resulting sample complexities incur a polynomial factor in the inverse-smallest eigenvalue, which we avoid. FID evaluation and debiasing. Since its introduction by [ 10 ] , FID has become a standard metric for evaluating generative models [ 23 , 30 , 12 ] . Despite its popularity, subsequent works identify limitations and propose improvements along two directions. One concerns finite-sample error , e.g., [ 7 ] propose FID ∞ \mathrm{FID}_{\infty} , which extrapolates FID scores in inverse sample size using a Taylor expansion heuristic. Their experiments support the underlying assumption and demonstrate empirical improvements, but they do not establish a rigorous finite-sample guarantee of FID ∞ \mathrm{FID}_{\infty} ’s improvement over the empirical plug-in estimator. Another direction questions the representational power of Inception embeddings. Prior work explores alternative representations such as CLIP [ 16 ] and DINOv2 [ 26 ] to address limitations of Inception features in perceptual evaluation. [ 12 ] empirically show that image distributions in Inception feature space are multimodal and violate the Gaussian assumption. They also expose FID’s vulnerability to manipulation: deliberately constructed image distortions can improve FID scores while degrading image quality. To mitigate this issue, MMD-based alternatives that avoid the Gaussian assumption have been proposed, including KID [ 4 ] and CMMD [ 12 ] . 2 Preliminaries 2.1 Notation General notation. We use ≲ \lesssim , ≳ \gtrsim , and ≍ \asymp to hide universal constants, and ≲ δ \lesssim_{\delta} , etc. to denote that the universal constant depends on a parameter δ \delta (also treated as a constant). We define [ d ] := { i ∈ ℕ : i ≤ d } [d]:=\{i\in\mathbb{N}:i\leq d\} . We denote vectors in either lowercase boldface or uppercase (italicized) boldface, and matrices in uppercase (upright) boldface. The identity matrix in ℝ d \mathbb{R}^{d} is denoted 𝐈 d \mathbf{I}_{d} , and the all-zeroes m × n m	imes n matrix is denoted 𝟎 m × n \mathbf{0}_{m	imes n} . We define the set of symmetric d × d d	imes d matrices by 𝕊 d × d \mathbb{S}^{d	imes d} with inner product ⟨ 𝐀 , 𝐁 ⟩ := Tr ​ ( 𝐀𝐁 ) \left\langle{\mathbf{A}},{\mathbf{B}}ightangle:=	extup{Tr}({\mathbf{A}}{\mathbf{B}}) , and the positive semidefinite (PSD) subset by 𝕊 ⪰ 𝟎 d × d \mathbb{S}_{\succeq\mathbf{0}}^{d	imes d} , equipped with the usual Loewner partial ordering, denoted by ⪯ \preceq . We use Tr ​ ( ⋅ ) 	extup{Tr}(\cdot) , ‖ ⋅ ‖ op \left\lVert\cdotightVert_{	extup{op}} , and ‖ ⋅ ‖ F \left\lVert\cdotightVert_{	extup{F}} to denote the trace, ℓ 2 \ell_{2} operator norm, and Frobenius norm of a matrix argument. We use ω < 2.373 \omega<2.373 to denote the exponent of d × d d	imes d matrix multiplication [ 1 ] . By standard reductions [ 27 , 22 ] , we also assume inversion and eigendecomposition take O ⁡ ( d ω ) O(d^{\omega}) time. Probability. We let 𝕀 ℰ \mathbb{I}_{{\mathcal{E}}} denote the 0 0 - 1 1 indicator function of an event ℰ {\mathcal{E}} . We use = 𝑑 \overset{d}{=} to denote equality in distribution. We let 𝒫 ⁡ ( Ω ) {\mathcal{P}}(\Omega) denote the set of probability measures over a sample space Ω \Omega , which we identify with their density functions when applicable. 